{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Six days, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to command this again. Further, why did it say <b>יֵעָשֶׂה</b> and not say תַּעֲשֶׂה, in the manner that it spoke on many other occasions. Further, why did it change here to mention it by the name <b>יוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי</b> ('the seventh day') when until now it had mentioned it only by the name שַׁבָּת, and further I see that it concluded by mentioning it with a change of name: <b>כָּל הָעוֹשֶׂה מְלָאכָה בְּיוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת</b> ('whoever does work on the day of the Sabbath'). Also, it should not have needed to say anything other than ״כִּי כָּל הָעוֹשֶׂה בוֹ״ ('for whoever does [work] on it')." "Or perhaps the verse alluded here to what they said in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 69a): The Sages taught — one was walking on the road or in the desert and does not know when Shabbat is — he counts six days and keeps one day. And they established in the Talmud that on each day he may do the livelihood-work of that day, even on Shabbat. And they asked: if so, by what is it recognizable? And they resolved: through kiddush and havdalah. And the Tosafot wrote that he should not walk on the day of Shabbat, and the reason the Talmud did not say that it is recognizable by [refraining from] walking is because that is not a recognizable sign of Shabbat, since even on the day he keeps he walks." "Now, according to the first approach, the verse may be explained in this manner: <b>Six days</b>, etc. — the verse speaks of one who was in the desert or at sea and does not know what day it is among the days. And it says <b>Six days יֵעָשֶׂה</b>, etc. — meaning, on them it is permitted to do work, to make something necessary which is the livelihood of that day. Therefore it did not say תֵּעָשֶׂה but <b>יֵעָשֶׂה</b> — meaning, he may do so to sustain his life. <b>And on the seventh day</b> — meaning, by his reckoning — <b>שַׁבַּת שַׁבָּתוֹן</b> — meaning, it is not truly Shabbat, but rather in the respect of שַׁבָּתוֹן as stated in the commandment of Shemitah, as it is written (Leviticus 25:4): ״שַׁבַּת שַׁבָּתוֹן יִהְיֶה לָאָרֶץ״ — that no work be done on the land. So too on the seventh day by his reckoning, he shall observe in it a cessation such that there is no walking on it. And it says <b>קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳</b> — that he must sanctify it with kiddush and havdalah as if it were the day of Shabbat." "And according to the approach that they explained — that there is no difference between the seventh day and the other six days except regarding kiddush and havdalah — it may be explained in this manner: <b>שַׁבַּת שַׁבָּתוֹן</b> in respect of <b>קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳</b> — meaning, to sanctify it, etc. And it says <b>כָּל הָעֹשֶׂה מְלָאכָה בְּיוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת מוֹת יוּמָת</b> — it was precise in saying 'on the day of the Sabbath' to exclude the seventh day of which the verse speaks, which is seventh by his reckoning, the day of his remembrance; for if he transgresses what he needs to be careful about on it, he is not liable, even if he does on it work that is not necessary — he is exempt, even though he sanctifies it under the assumption that it is the day of Shabbat."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:11.597981253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}